.TH CREW 1 "2026-10-02" "sc-fleet 0.1.0" "sc-fleet manual"
.SH NAME
crew \- every Claude session you have running, and what it is doing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBcrew\fR
\fBcrew\fR \fB-a\fR | \fB--all\fR
\fBcrew\fR \fB--sample\fR
\fBcrew\fR \fB--porcelain\fR
\fBcrew doctor\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Ten sessions across several boxes and no way to see them: which project each
is in, which have gone quiet, which is burning CPU, which spawned subagents.
.PP
\fBtmux list-panes\fR shows a pane running \fBclaude\fR and nothing more,
because the interesting state is one process further down and in the
transcript file. \fBcrew\fR assembles it into one roll call.
.PP
Everything it reads is read-only, and a session outside tmux still appears:
discovery starts from the process list, not from tmux, and the pane column
reads \fB-\fR when there is no pane.
.SH WHERE THE FACTS COME FROM
.TP
.B session
\fBpgrep -x claude\fR, then the tmux pane whose shell is an ancestor of that
process. The walk up the parent chain is necessary because \fBclaude\fR is a
child of the pane's shell, so a pane's own pid never matches directly.
.TP
.B project
\fI/proc/<pid>/cwd\fR.
.TP
.B session id
The transcript filename under \fI~/.claude/projects/<slug>/\fR, where the
slug is the working directory with every non-alphanumeric run replaced by a
dash.
.TP
.B idle
The transcript's mtime. Claude appends to it every turn.
.TP
.B subagents
Transcripts under \fI<session>/subagents/\fR modified in the last 30
minutes.
.TP
.B cpu, rss, up
\fI/proc/<pid>/stat\fR and \fI/proc/<pid>/status\fR, against
\fI/proc/stat\fR's btime for the uptime.
.SH STATE, AND ITS ONE HONEST LIMITATION
State is derived from how long ago the transcript was last appended to:
.PP
.RS
.nf
active          under 45 seconds
waiting         under 30 minutes
quiet           under a day
stale           older
no-transcript   the project dir has none
.fi
.RE
.PP
A transcript is appended per \fIturn\fR, so a session in the middle of a long
tool call or a long thought looks \fBwaiting\fR when it is working hard.
\fB--sample\fR resolves it properly by measuring a CPU delta over 0.6
seconds and relabelling the row \fBbusy\fR. That distinction is not
cosmetic: the first real run of this tool showed a session at three minutes
idle that \fB--sample\fR proved was busy.
.PP
\fB--sample\fR costs a fraction of a second per box, so it is opt-in rather
than the default.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS crew
This box. One row per session, newest activity coloured as yours.
.SS crew -a, crew --all
Every box in the shared registry, queried read-only over ssh. The probe is
this same script piped in on stdin to \fBbash -s\fR, so \fBcrew\fR does not
need to be installed on the far side. Boxes are rendered in registry order,
and a box that times out or refuses is marked transient rather than
aborting the run.
.PP
With no registry, this degrades to the local roll call and says so. It never
refuses for missing data.
.SS crew --sample
Measure a CPU delta over 0.6 seconds per session and relabel a session that
is genuinely working as \fBbusy\fR, instead of trusting transcript age.
Opt-in because it costs a fraction of a second per box.
.SS crew --porcelain
Tab-separated, no header, no colour, no art: box, pane, project, full
session id, state, idle seconds, uptime seconds, cpu seconds, rss MB,
subagent count. Raw seconds rather than the human form, because the
consumer can format and cannot un-format.
.SS crew doctor
Whether sessions can be seen from here at all: \fI/proc\fR, \fBpgrep\fR, a
tmux server, the projects directory, and the registry.
.SH REGISTRY
\fBcrew -a\fR reads the same roster as \fBrig\fR(1),
\fI~/.config/rig/boxes.conf\fR, deliberately: one roster, not two. A box
needs an \fBssh=\fR alias to be contacted; a stanza without one is listed as
registry-only. The registry is site data and is never part of the tool.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CREW_PROJECTS
Where session transcripts live. Default \fI~/.claude/projects\fR.
.TP
.B RIG_BOXES
The registry path, shared with \fBrig\fR(1).
.TP
.B CREW_TIMEOUT \fRor\fB RIG_TIMEOUT
Per-box ssh timeout in seconds. Default 12.
.TP
.B CREW_PAR \fRor\fB RIG_PAR
Concurrent ssh probes. Default 6.
.TP
.B RIG_SSH_OPTS
Appended to every ssh invocation, so a jump host can be added without the
tool knowing one exists.
.TP
.B CREW_ART
Force the banner even into a pipe.
.TP
.B CREW_NO_ART \fRor\fB NO_COLOR
Suppress art, or colour.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/crew
The tool, in the sc-fleet checkout, which is also the remote probe.
.TP
.I ~/.config/rig/boxes.conf
The roster. Site data, delivered by symlink, never in the tool's repo.
.TP
.I tests/test_crew.sh
The test suite. Run: \fBbash tests/test_crew.sh\fR
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the roll call was rendered, including when some boxes were
unreachable, since unreachability is transient. 1 on a refusal.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS
crew                       # what am I running here
crew --sample              # and which of them is actually working
crew -a                    # across every box in the roster
crew --porcelain | wc -l   # how many sessions am I paying for
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sc (1),
.BR rig (1),
.BR arm (1),
.BR wt (1)
